# Mathematical induction

**Mathematical induction** is a proof scheme: if a statement holds for zero,
and holds for $n+1$ whenever it holds for $n$, then it holds for every natural
number. Despite the name it is a form of [[deduction]]. Its conclusion is
certain, it uses no observations, and it lives entirely inside a formal
theory. It is a different concept from [[induction]] as inference from
evidence.

## Description

The scheme has two premises and one conclusion.

$$\bigl(P(0)\ \wedge\ \forall n\,(P(n)\rightarrow P(n+1))\bigr)\ \rightarrow\ \forall n\,P(n).$$

In first-order arithmetic this is an axiom schema, one axiom for each formula
$P$. A proof by induction is an ordinary derivation that uses one of those
axioms. Nothing in it goes beyond what the theory already contains, which is
the sense in which it is deductive.

It belongs with the step-by-step constructions that the discussion placed
"before recursion": repeating one operation, as in the first, second and third
derivative, or lifting an operation to the next one, as addition gives
multiplication and multiplication gives exponentiation. Those are things built
one stage at a time. Mathematical induction is how one proves something about
everything built that way.

Its twin is [[recursion]]. Recursion defines an object for $n+1$ from the
object for $n$. Induction proves a property for $n+1$ from the property for
$n$. The two have the same shape, and each needs a base case to stand on.

The scheme extends past the natural numbers. Transfinite induction runs along
the ordinals and adds a third case for limit stages. The
[[constructible-universe]] is the example in this vault: its hierarchy is
defined by transfinite recursion, successor stage by successor stage with
unions at the limits, and every fact about it is proved by transfinite
induction along the same stages.

Because it works strictly downward from a base, mathematical induction is
well-founded. It does not loop, and it is one of the standard anti-examples
when asking what counts as a [[strange-loop]].

## Relations

- It is a form of [[deduction]] {relation: is-a}, not of inference from evidence.
- It is a different concept from [[induction]] {relation: distinguished-from}, with which it shares only the name.
- It is the proof-side twin of [[recursion]] {relation: analogous-to}: one defines stage by stage, the other proves stage by stage.
- Its transfinite form is the working tool of the [[constructible-universe]] {relation: uses}.
- It is not a [[strange-loop]] {relation: distinguished-from}: it is well-founded and crosses no level.
- It is one of the axioms assumed when stating the [[incompleteness-theorems]] {relation: part-of} for arithmetic.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[induction]].** Inference from observations to a hypothesis that may
  turn out false, always paired with deduction in a cycle. Mathematical
  induction is on the deduction side of that pairing.
- **[[recursion]].** Definition of a thing through a smaller instance of
  itself, ending at a base case. It is the construction that mathematical
  induction reasons about.
- **[[constructible-universe]].** Gödel's hierarchy of sets built by taking
  definable subsets at each stage. Every stage is reached, and every property
  proved, by transfinite recursion and induction.

